{
  "analysis": "The chronological move reconstruction is still not working well - there are duplicates, OCR errors, and partial commands. The approach of tracking new commands between frames is flawed because the same command text appears in multiple frames (scrolling text) and the comparison logic isn't handling this correctly. I need a fundamentally different approach.

The key insight is that the Zork terminal shows the current command being typed at the bottom `>` prompt. As the player types, the text grows character by character. When they press Enter, the command is executed and a new `>` prompt appears. So I should track the LAST `>` line in each frame (the one being typed), and when it changes from one frame to the next, record the previous complete command.

Let me also try a better approach: extract the bottom portion of each frame (just the current command line) at higher resolution and OCR it specifically. But first, let me try the last-command tracking approach with proper chronological ordering.

Actually, let me reconsider. The real problem is that I have 191 frames over 190 seconds, and the video shows a Zork playthrough. The game text scrolls, so each frame shows a different view. I need to track the bottom `>` prompt line across frames to see what's being typed.

Let me write a better script that:
1. Sorts frames numerically
2. For each frame, extracts only the LAST `>` line (the current input being typed)
3. Groups consecutive frames where the last command grows
4. Records the longest version before a new command starts

But I also need to handle the case where the last `>` line is a new prompt (empty) after a command is submitted. Let me look at the actual frame data more carefully first.{
  "analysis": "The chronological reconstruction is still producing duplicates and errors. The core issue is that I need to track the LAST `>` prompt line (the current input being typed) across frames in chronological order, not all `>` lines. When the last line changes from a command to empty/new, the previous command was completed. Let me first look at the actual per-frame last command lines in chronological order to understand the pattern.",
  "plan": "Write a Python script that sorts frames numerically and prints the last `>` line for each frame in chronological order. This will show the typing progression and help me build a correct reconstruction algorithm.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "python3 - <<'PY'\nimport re\ntext=open('/app/ocr_1s.txt').read()\nparts=re.split(r'===== (\\d+) =====', text)\nframes=[]\nfor k in range(1,len(parts),2):\n    t=int(parts[k])\n    content=parts[k+1].strip()\n    lines=content.splitlines()\n    cmds=[l[1:].strip() for l in lines if l.startswith('>')]\n    last=cmds[-1] if cmds else ''\n    m=re.search(r'Moves:\\s*(\\d+)', content)\n    mc=int(m.group(1)) if m else None\n    frames.append((t,last,mc))\nframes.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\nwith open('/app/last_cmd_chrono.txt','w') as f:\n    for t,last,mc in frames:\n        f.write(f't={t:3d} Moves={str(mc):>4s} last={last!r}\\n')\nPY\ncat /app/last_cmd_chrono.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}
